                   . Accessed 5 June 2021" href="https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s10639-024-12602-5#ref-CR162" id="ref-link-section-d81076584e1223">b</a>).</p><div class="c-article-section__figure js-c-reading-companion-figures-item" data-test="figure" data-container-section="figure" id="figure-5" data-title="Fig. 5"><figure><figcaption><b id="Fig5" class="c-article-section__figure-caption" data-test="figure-caption-text">Fig. 5</b></figcaption><div class="c-article-section__figure-content"><div class="c-article-section__figure-item"><picture class="c-article-section__figure-picture"><source type="image/webp" srcset="//media.springernature.com/lw685/springer-static/image/art%3A10.1007%2Fs10639-024-12602-5/MediaObjects/10639_2024_12602_Fig5_HTML.png?as=webp"><img aria-describedby="figure-5-desc" src="https://media.springernature.com/lw685/springer-static/image/art%3A10.1007%2Fs10639-024-12602-5/MediaObjects/10639_2024_12602_Fig5_HTML.png" alt="Fig. 5" loading="lazy" width="685" height="418"></picture><div class="c-article-section__figure-link"><a class="c-article__pill-button" data-test="article-link" data-track="click" data-track-label="button" data-track-action="view figure" href="https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s10639-024-12602-5/figures/5" data-track-dest="link:Figure5 Full size image" aria-label="Full size image figure 5" rel="nofollow"><span>Full size image</span><svg width="16" height="16" focusable="false" role="img" aria-hidden="true" class="u-icon"><use xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xlink:href="#icon-eds-i-chevron-right-small"></use></svg></a></div></div><div class="c-article-section__figure-description" data-test="bottom-caption" id="figure-5-desc"><p>VR distribution</p></div></div></figure></div><div class="c-article-section__figure js-c-reading-companion-figures-item" data-test="figure" data-container-section="figure" id="figure-6" data-title="Fig. 6"><figure><figcaption><b id="Fig6" class="c-article-section__figure-caption" data-test="figure-caption-text">Fig. 6</b></figcaption><div class="c-article-section__figure-content"><div class="c-article-section__figure-item"><picture class="c-article-section__figure-picture"><source type="image/webp" srcset="//media.springernature.com/lw685/springer-static/image/art%3A10.1007%2Fs10639-024-12602-5/MediaObjects/10639_2024_12602_Fig6_HTML.png?as=webp"><img aria-describedby="figure-6-desc" src="https://media.springernature.com/lw685/springer-static/image/art%3A10.1007%2Fs10639-024-12602-5/MediaObjects/10639_2024_12602_Fig6_HTML.png" alt="Fig. 6" loading="lazy" width="685" height="422"></picture><div class="c-article-section__figure-link"><a class="c-article__pill-button" data-test="article-link" data-track="click" data-track-label="button" data-track-action="view figure" href="https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s10639-024-12602-5/figures/6" data-track-dest="link:Figure6 Full size image" aria-label="Full size image figure 6" rel="nofollow"><span>Full size image</span><svg width="16" height="16" focusable="false" role="img" aria-hidden="true" class="u-icon"><use xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xlink:href="#icon-eds-i-chevron-right-small"></use></svg></a></div></div><div class="c-article-section__figure-description" data-test="bottom-caption" id="figure-6-desc"><p>AR distribution</p></div></div></figure></div><p>In this research, the authors also identified that AR technology adoption at the K-12 level was higher than VR technology adoption (see Fig.&nbsp;<a data-track="click" data-track-label="link" data-track-action="figure anchor" href="https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s10639-024-12602-5#Fig4">4</a>). AR technology does not require expensive hardware and sophisticated equipment like VR technology. It can be utilized with computers or handheld devices, making AR technology used easily at every level of K-12 (AkÃ§ayÄ±r &amp; AkÃ§ayÄ±r, <a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 2017" title="AkÃ§ayÄ±r, M., &amp; AkÃ§ayÄ±r, G. (2017). Advantages and challenges associated with augmented reality for education: A systematic review of the literature. Educational Research Review, 20, 1â€“11. 
                  https://doi.org/10.1016/j.edurev.2016.11.002
                  
                " href="https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s10639-024-12602-5#ref-CR6" id="ref-link-section-d81076584e1267">2017</a>). The available educational VR content is often designed for stand-alone learning experiences. Further, it was not designed as a tool to be utilized in different educational systems and with different pedagogical approaches. Learning the functionality of VR devices and understanding each applicationâ€™s logic can also consume time for educators and learners. Thus, it makes the VR content less suitable for classroom use (Fransson et al., <a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 2020" title="Fransson, G., Holmberg, J., &amp; Westelius, C. (2020). The challenges of using head mounted virtual reality in K-12 schools from a teacher perspective. Education and Information Technologies, 25(4), 3383â€“3404. 
                  https://doi.org/10.1007/s10639-020-10119-1
                  
                " href="https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s10639-024-12602-5#ref-CR66" id="ref-link-section-d81076584e1270">2020</a>; Jensen &amp; Konradsen, <a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 2018" title="Jensen, L., &amp; Konradsen, F. (2018). A review of the use of virtual reality head-mounted displays in education and training. Education and Information Technologies, 23(4), 1515â€“1529. 
                  https://doi.org/10.1007/s10639-017-9676-0
                  
                " href="https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s10639-024-12602-5#ref-CR92" id="ref-link-section-d81076584e1273">2018</a>). Accordingly, this may explain why AR technology reached a high level of adoption compared to VR technology in the K-12 educational system.</p><p>It was observed in this study that VR technology integration was noticeably higher in the industrial training educational system in contrast to AR. VR technology is more immersive and attractive via spatial presence than AR apps (Huang et al., <a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 2019" title="Huang, K. T., Ball, C., Francis, J., Ratan, R., Boumis, J., &amp; Fordham, J. (2019). Augmented versus virtual reality in education: An exploratory study examining science knowledge retention when using augmented reality/virtual reality mobile applications. Cyberpsychology Behavior and Social Networking, 22(2), 105â€“110. 
                  https://doi.org/10.1089/cyber.2018.0150
                  
                " href="https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s10639-024-12602-5#ref-CR87" id="ref-link-section-d81076584e1279">2019</a>). That was evident in the mining industry (Liang et al., <a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 2019" title="Liang, Z., Zhou, K., &amp; Gao, K. (2019). Development of virtual reality serious game for underground rock-related hazards safety training. IEEE Accessâ€¯: Practical Innovations, Open Solutions, 7, 118639â€“118649. 
