                  https://doi.org/10.1080/10494820.2020.1841799
                  
                " href="https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s10639-024-12602-5#ref-CR109" id="ref-link-section-d81076584e2228">2020</a>). It improves the learnersâ€™ learning performance and enhances their satisfaction with the learning process (Fajrianti et al., <a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 2021" title="Fajrianti, E. D., Sukaridhoto, S., Al Rasyid, M. U. H., Budiarti, R. P. N., Hafidz, A., Satrio, I. A., N. A., &amp; Firmanda, A. (2021). Design and development of human anatomy learning platform for medical students based on augmented intelligence technology. In 2021 International Electronics Symposium (IES) (pp. 195â€“202). IEEE. 
                  https://doi.org/10.1109/IES53407.2021.9594053
                  
                " href="https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s10639-024-12602-5#ref-CR62" id="ref-link-section-d81076584e2231">2021</a>; Henssen et al., <a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 2020" title="Henssen, D. J., van den Heuvel, L., De Jong, G., Vorstenbosch, M. A., van Cappellen, A. M., Van den Hurk, M. M., &amp; Bartels, R. H. (2020). Neuroanatomy learning: Augmented reality vs. cross-sections. Anatomical Sciences Education, 13(3), 353â€“365. 
                  https://doi.org/10.1002/ase.1912
                  
                " href="https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s10639-024-12602-5#ref-CR82" id="ref-link-section-d81076584e2234">2020</a>). However, we observed that satisfaction with using VR and AR technologies requires more investigation in industrial training.</p><p>It also develops a positive attitude during the learning experience (Bursali &amp; Yilmaz, <a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 2019" title="Bursali, H., &amp; Yilmaz, R. M. (2019). Effect of augmented reality applications on secondary school studentsâ€™ reading comprehension and learning permanency. Computers in Human Behavior, 95, 126â€“135. 
                  https://doi.org/10.1016/j.chb.2019.01.035
                  
                " href="https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s10639-024-12602-5#ref-CR37" id="ref-link-section-d81076584e2240">2019</a>; Papakostas et al., <a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 2021" title="Papakostas, C., Troussas, C., Krouska, A., &amp; Sgouropoulou, C. (2021). Exploration of augmented reality in spatial abilities training: A systematic literature review for the last decade. Informatics in Education, 20(1), 107â€“130. 
                  https://doi.org/10.15388/infedu.2021.06
                  
                " href="https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s10639-024-12602-5#ref-CR138" id="ref-link-section-d81076584e2243">2021</a>). VR and AR educational-based experiences are vivid experiences that attract different types of learners, whether young or adult learners, and indirectly raise concentration levels, especially for young learners during the learning process (Ting et al., <a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 2021" title="Ting, G., Jianmin, W., Yongning, Z., &amp; Qiuyu, C. (2021). Research on interaction design of chemical inquiry virtual experiment based on augmented reality technology. In 2021 IEEE 7th International Conference on Virtual Reality (ICVR) (pp. 340â€“351). IEEE.  
                  https://doi.org/10.1109/ICVR51878.2021.9483706
                  
                " href="https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s10639-024-12602-5#ref-CR169" id="ref-link-section-d81076584e2246">2021</a>). The findings of (Gargrish et al., <a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 2020" title="Gargrish, S., Mantri, A., &amp; Singh, G. (2020). Measuring studentsâ€™ motivation towards virtual reality game-like learning environments. In 2020 Indoâ€“Taiwan 2nd International Conference on Computing, Analytics and Networks (Indo-Taiwan ICAN) (pp. 164â€“169). IEEE.  
                  https://doi.org/10.1109/Indo-TaiwanICAN48429.2020.9181362
                  
                " href="https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s10639-024-12602-5#ref-CR70" id="ref-link-section-d81076584e2249">2020</a>) study on secondary education students showed that immersive capabilities aid young learners in raising their concentration level and memorizing concepts for a long time. Further, it has been found that VR and AR assist in developing imagination and communicative skills (Khan et al., <a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 2021b" title="Khan, N., Muhammad, K., Hussain, T., Nasir, M., Munsif, M., Imran, A. S., &amp; Sajjad, M. (2021b). An adaptive game-based learning strategy for children road safety education and practice in virtual space. Sensors (Basel, Switzerland), 21(11), 3661. 
                  https://doi.org/10.3390/s21113661
                  
                " href="https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s10639-024-12602-5#ref-CR102" id="ref-link-section-d81076584e2252">2021b</a>;&nbsp;Schott et al., <a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 2021" title="Schott, D., Saalfeld, P., Schmidt, G., Joeres, F., Boedecker, C., Huettl, F., ... &amp; Hansen, C. (2021). A vr/ar environment for multi-user liver anatomy education. In 2021 IEEE Virtual Reality and 3D User Interfaces (VR) (pp. 296â€“305). IEEE. 
                  https://doi.org/10.1109/VR50410.2021.00052
                  
                " href="https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s10639-024-12602-5#ref-CR155" id="ref-link-section-d81076584e2256">2021</a>) for K-12 and HE learners, respectively. Unleashing the imagination, especially among curious young learners, is crucial as it develops creativity and problem-solving skills. Imagination can also lead to establishing young scientists with the help of VR and AR technologies as it allows them to perform dangerous and complicated tasks that are difficult to execute in a real environment. It also assists in boosting social and communicative skills. Armah and Landers-Potts (<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 2021" title="Armah, A., &amp; Landers-Potts, M. (2021). A review of imaginary companions and their implications for development. Imagination Cognition and Personality, 41(1), 31â€“53. 
                  https://doi.org/10.1177/0276236621999324
                  
                " href="https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s10639-024-12602-5#ref-CR15" id="ref-link-section-d81076584e2259">2021</a>) have affirmed that children with imagination are more social compared to other children who do not possess such skills. Imagination skills have been positively linked to cognitive skills, which are usually considered beneficial for social development.</p><p>VR and AR apps also boost collaboration between learners (Wang et al., <a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 2018" title="Wang, P., Wu, P., Wang, J., Chi, H. L., &amp; Wang, X. (2018). A critical review of the use of virtual reality in construction engineering education and training. International Journal of Environmental Research and Public Health, 15(6), 1204. 
                  https://doi.org/10.3390/ijerph15061204
                  
                " href="https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s10639-024-12602-5#ref-CR175" id="ref-link-section-d81076584e2265">2018</a>; Zhu et al., <a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 2021" title="Zhu, Y., Lou, Z., Ge, T., Wu, T., Wang, Y., Tan, T., &amp; Wang, J. (2021). An interactive mixed reality platform for inquiry-based education. In 2021 IEEE 7th International Conference on Virtual Reality (ICVR) (pp. 324â€“331). IEEE.  
                  https://doi.org/10.1109/ICVR51878.2021.9483827
                  
                " href="https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s10639-024-12602-5#ref-CR190" id="ref-link-section-d81076584e2268">2021</a>). It can allow many learners to interact with the same app simultaneously across various educational systems, which enables them to share their suggestions and opinions. As a result, this will improve inventive thinking (Badge, <a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 2021" title="Badge, S. (2021). Introduction of augmented reality and virtual reality in the growing education sector: A review. International Journal of Modern Agriculture, 10(2), 181â€“188." href="https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s10639-024-12602-5#ref-CR24" id="ref-link-section-d81076584e2271">2021</a>; Lamb et al., <a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 2018" title="Lamb, R., Antonenko, P., Etopio, E., &amp; Seccia, A. (2018). Comparison of virtual reality and hands on activities in science education via functional near infrared spectroscopy. Computers &amp; Education, 124, 14â€“26. 
                  https://doi.org/10.1016/j.compedu.2018.05.014
                  
                " href="https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s10639-024-12602-5#ref-CR108" id="ref-link-section-d81076584e2274">2018</a>). This collaboration will not only assist learners in sharing and acquiring knowledge from other learners. However, it can also prepare and qualify learners for complicated tasks and estimate the taskâ€™s success if it is executed in a real environment. It also can predict what might be faced if the same procedures are followed in a real environment. In the medical industry, Venkatesan et al. (<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 2021" title="Venkatesan, M., Mohan, H., Ryan, J. R., SchÃ¼rch, C. M., Nolan, G. P., Frakes, D. H., &amp; Coskun, A. F. (2021). Virtual and augmented reality for biomedical applications. Cell Reports Medicine, 2(7), 100348. 
                  https://doi.org/10.1016/j.xcrm.2021.100348
                  
                " href="https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s10639-024-12602-5#ref-CR174" id="ref-link-section-d81076584e2277">2021</a>) stated that VR allows medical learners and participants to practice the required procedures before performing surgery on an individual to address the required issue. For example, a group of surgeons in separate countries specializing in conjoined twin separations have collaborated through VR for months to trial techniques to perform one of the most complex separation processes. The surgeons have collaborated using VR projections of the twins based on CT and MRI scans. For the first time, VR technology has enabled surgeons to wear headsets and operate in the same Virtual Reality room (McCallum, <a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 2022" title="McCallum, S. (2022). Conjoined twins separated with the help of virtual reality. BBC News. Retrieved from 
                  https://www.bbc.com/news/technology-62378452
                  
                . Accessed 21 Jan 2023" href="https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s10639-024-12602-5#ref-CR122" id="ref-link-section-d81076584e2281">2022</a>). VR collaboration increased the success rate of complex separation processes while removing the risk factor and facilitating sharing knowledge and opinions on such operations. Further, it makes surgeons predict what they might encounter during the real separation processes. Consequently, sharing knowledge is not the only gain from collaboration via VR and AR. However, raising prediction and success levels, especially for critical tasks, may be considered new gains.</p><p>This study also discovered that VR and AR learning content raises the interactivity level with the learning materials (Ahmed et al., <a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 2021" title="Ahmed, N., Lataifeh, M., Alhamarna, A. F., Alnahdi, M. M., &amp; Almansori, S. T. (2021). LeARn: A collaborative learning environment using augmented reality. In 2021 IEEE 2nd International Conference on Human-Machine Systems (ICHMS) (pp. 1â€“4). IEEE.  
                  https://doi.org/10.1109/ICHMS53169.2021.9582643
                  
                " href="https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s10639-024-12602-5#ref-CR4" id="ref-link-section-d81076584e2287">2021</a>). Furthermore, it allows participants to learn and work in a low-risk and safe environment (Doolani et al., <a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 2020" title="Doolani, S., Wessels, C., Kanal, V., Sevastopoulos, C., Jaiswal, A., Nambiappan, H., &amp; Makedon, F. (2020). A review of extended reality (xr) technologies for manufacturing training. Technologies, 8(4), 77. 
                  https://doi.org/10.3390/technologies8040077
                  
                " href="https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s10639-024-12602-5#ref-CR56" id="ref-link-section-d81076584e2290">2020</a>). This is considered a great feature for young learners, such as K-12 and adult learners, such as vocational and industrial learners. Working in a safe environment is crucial since it enables industrial trainees and vocational learners to learn and practice the necessary tasks numerous times without concern. Consequently, it aids in the transformation of learners into workplace workers (Guo, <a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 2019" title="Guo, X. (2019). Research on accounting teaching based on virtual reality technology. In 2019 International Conference on Virtual Reality and Intelligent Systems (ICVRIS) (pp. 36â€“39). IEEE.  
                  https://doi.org/10.1109/ICVRIS.2019.00017
                  
                " href="https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s10639-024-12602-5#ref-CR77" id="ref-link-section-d81076584e2293">2019</a>).</p><p>The safe environment provided by VR and AR educational-based applications helped reduce errors and task time for accomplishing complicated tasks (Radosavljevic et al., <a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 2020" title="Radosavljevic, S., Radosavljevic, V., &amp; Grgurovic, B. (2020). The potential of implementing augmented reality into vocational higher education through mobile learning. Interactive Learning Environments, 28(4), 404â€“418. 
                  https://doi.org/10.1080/10494820.2018.1528286
                  
                " href="https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s10639-024-12602-5#ref-CR144" id="ref-link-section-d81076584e2300">2020</a>; Zhang et al., <a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 2021" title="Zhang, Y. X., Cheng, J. Q., Wang, J. Y., &amp; Zhao, L. (2021). Co-assemble: A collaborative AR cross-devices teaching system for assemble practice courses. In 2021 IEEE Conference on Virtual Reality and 3D User Interfaces Abstracts and Workshops (VRW) (pp. 512â€“513). IEEE.  
                  https://doi.org/10.1109/VRW52623.2021.00138
                  
                " href="https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s10639-024-12602-5#ref-CR187" id="ref-link-section-d81076584e2303">2021</a>). It allows learners to feel more comfortable when learning with virtual 3D machines, and learners who use the VR and AR app outperform those who use traditional methods to accomplish the same tasks. VR and AR apps have improved technical skills for higher education, vocational education, and industrial training learners (Guo et al., <a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 2020" title="Guo, Z., Zhou, D., Zhou, Q., Zhang, X., Geng, J., Zeng, S., &amp; Hao, A. (2020). Applications of virtual reality in maintenance during the industrial product lifecycle: A systematic review. Journal of Manufacturing Systems, 56, 525â€“538. 
                  https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jmsy.2020.07.007
                  
                " href="https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s10639-024-12602-5#ref-CR78" id="ref-link-section-d81076584e2306">2020</a>; Nikitin et al., <a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 2020" title="Nikitin, A., Reshetnikova, N., Sitnikov, I., &amp; Karelova, O. (2020). VR training for railway wagons maintenance: architecture and implementation. Procedia Computer Science, 176, 622â€“631. 
                  https://doi.org/10.1016/j.procs.2020.08.064
                  
                " href="https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s10639-024-12602-5#ref-CR133" id="ref-link-section-d81076584e2309">2020</a>;  Shamsuzzoha et al., <a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 2021" title="Shamsuzzoha, A., Toshev, R., Vu Tuan, V., Kankaanpaa, T., &amp; Helo, P. (2021). Digital factoryâ€“virtual reality environments for industrial training and maintenance. Interactive Learning Environments, 29(8), 1339â€“1362. 
                  https://doi.org/10.1080/10494820.2019.1628072
                  
                " href="https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s10639-024-12602-5#ref-CR157" id="ref-link-section-d81076584e2312">2021</a>). In addition, it aids in identifying hazards in safety training, thus raising awareness of hazardous situations (Hoang et al., <a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 2021" title="Hoang, T., Greuter, S., Taylor, S., Aranda, G., &amp; Mulvany, G. T. (2021). An evaluation of virtual reality for fear arousal safety training in the construction industry. In 2021 IEEE International Symposium on Mixed and Augmented Reality Adjunct (ISMAR-Adjunct) (pp. 177â€“182). IEEE.  
                  https://doi.org/10.1109/ISMAR-Adjunct54149.2021.00044
                  
                " href="https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s10639-024-12602-5#ref-CR83" id="ref-link-section-d81076584e2316">2021</a>). Such a feature significantly improves safety training as it helps save workers who are used to working in dangerous sites. For instance, in the mining industry (Liang et al., <a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 2019" title="Liang, Z., Zhou, K., &amp; Gao, K. (2019). Development of virtual reality serious game for underground rock-related hazards safety training. IEEE Accessâ€¯: Practical Innovations, Open Solutions, 7, 118639â€“118649. 
                  https://doi.org/10.1109/ACCESS.2019.2934990
                  
                " href="https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s10639-024-12602-5#ref-CR112" id="ref-link-section-d81076584e2319">2019</a>). In this research, the authors also found that VR and AR educational apps can decrease the learnersâ€™ need for learning with physical elements (Liu et al., <a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 2021a" title="Liu, Y., Liu, Y., &amp; Yue, K. (2021a). Integration of concept maps into the mixed reality learning space: Quasi-experimental design and preliminary results. In IEEE Conference on Virtual Reality and 3D User Interfaces Abstracts and Workshops (VRW), 2021, pp. 627â€“628. 
                  https://doi.org/10.1109/VRW52623.2021.00196
                  
                " href="https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s10639-024-12602-5#ref-CR114" id="ref-link-section-d81076584e2322">2021a</a>).</p><h4 class="c-article__sub-heading c-article__sub-heading--small" id="Sec17"><span class="c-article-section__title-number">4.4.2 </span>Technology effects on educators</h4><p>The most noticeable effect on educators was â€œAssist in teachingâ€ (see Table&nbsp;<a data-track="click" data-track-label="link" data-track-action="table anchor" href="https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s10639-024-12602-5#Tab4">4</a>) (Coimbra et al., <a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 2015" title="Coimbra, M. T., Cardoso, T., &amp; Mateus, A. (2015). Augmented reality: An enhancer for higher education students in mathâ€™s learning? Procedia Computer Science, 67, 332â€“339. 
                  https://doi.org/10.1016/j.procs.2015.09.277
                  
                " href="https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s10639-024-12602-5#ref-CR46" id="ref-link-section-d81076584e2336">2015</a>; Sahin &amp; Yilmaz, <a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 2020" title="Sahin, D., &amp; Yilmaz, R. M. (2020). The effect of augmented reality technology on middle school studentsâ€™ achievements and attitudes towards science education. Computers &amp; Education, 144, 103710. 
                  https://doi.org/10.1016/j.compedu.2019.103710
                  
                " href="https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s10639-024-12602-5#ref-CR149" id="ref-link-section-d81076584e2339">2020</a>; Scavarelli et al., <a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 2020" title="Scavarelli, A., Arya, A., &amp; Teather, R. J. (2020). Virtual reality and augmented reality in social learning spaces: A literature review. Virtual Reality, 25, 257â€“277. 
                  https://doi.org/10.1007/s10055-020-00444-8
                  
                " href="https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s10639-024-12602-5#ref-CR153" id="ref-link-section-d81076584e2342">2020</a>; Sharma et al., <a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 2021" title="Sharma, B., Singh, N. P., Mantri, A., Gargrish, S., Tuli, N., &amp; Sharma, S. (2021). Save the earth: Teaching environment studies using augmented reality. In 2021 6th International Conference on Signal Processing, Computing and Control (ISPCC) (pp. 336â€“339). IEEE.  
                  https://doi.org/10.1109/ISPCC53510.2021.9609463
                  
                " href="https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s10639-024-12602-5#ref-CR158" id="ref-link-section-d81076584e2345">2021</a>; VÃ¡zquez-Cano et al., <a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 2020" title="VÃ¡zquez-Cano, E., MarÃ­n-DÃ­az, V., Oyarvide, W. R. V., &amp; Meneses, E. L. (2020). Use of augmented reality to improve specific and transversal competenciess in students. International Journal of Learning, Teaching and Educational Research, 19, 393â€“408. 
                  https://doi.org/10.26803/ijlter.19.8.21
                  
                " href="https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s10639-024-12602-5#ref-CR173" id="ref-link-section-d81076584e2349">2020</a>). With the integration of VR and AR, the role of educators will witness a radical change. Educators will transform from content delivery to content facilitators. The 3D virtual elements in VR and AR apps can deliver the learning content effectively with excellent guidance. Also, integrating virtual avatars with VR and AR apps can become a private educator (Harborth &amp; KÃ¼mpers, <a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 2021" title="Harborth, D., &amp; KÃ¼mpers, K. (2021). Intelligence augmentation: Rethinking the future of work by leveraging human performance and abilities. Virtual Reality, 1â€“22. 
                  https://doi.org/10.1007/s10055-021-00590-7
                  
                " href="https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s10639-024-12602-5#ref-CR80" id="ref-link-section-d81076584e2352">2021</a>; Wei et al., <a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 2020" title="Wei, X., Yang, G., Zhang, K., &amp; Li, Z. (2020). Research on mobile ar language learning environment based on virtual avatar. In 2020 Ninth International Conference of Educational Innovation through Technology (EITT) (pp. 229â€“234). IEEE.  
                  https://doi.org/10.1109/EITT50754.2020.00047
                  
                " href="https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s10639-024-12602-5#ref-CR177" id="ref-link-section-d81076584e2355">2020</a>), as it plays the role of the educator in guiding learners during the learning experience. As a result, the burden on educators and trainers is reduced (Abidi et al., <a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 2019" title="Abidi, M. H., Al-Ahmari, A., Ahmad, A., Ameen, W., &amp; Alkhalefah, H. (2019). Assessment of virtual reality-based manufacturing assembly training system. The International Journal of Advanced Manufacturing Technology, 105(9), 3743â€“3759. 
                  https://doi.org/10.1007/s00170-019-03801-3
                  
                " href="https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s10639-024-12602-5#ref-CR1" id="ref-link-section-d81076584e2358">2019</a>; Bistaman et al., <a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 2018" title="Bistaman, I. N. M., Idrus, S. Z. S., &amp; Abd Rashid, S. (2018). The use of augmented reality technology for primary school education in Perlis, Malaysia. In Journal of Physics: Conference Series (Vol. 1019, No. 1, pp. 012064). IOP Publishing.  
                  https://doi.org/10.1088/1742-6596/1019/1/012064
                  
                " href="https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s10639-024-12602-5#ref-CR30" id="ref-link-section-d81076584e2361">2018</a>). Reducing the burden on educators depends on setting their goals from using VR and AR as teaching assistance tools and identifying learnersâ€™ needs before using technology. To clarify, educators should fully grasp what resources, tools, and technology types will serve learnersâ€™ needs and help them understand the learning content better. For instance, it is wise to select VR resources with minimum motion if most learners experience some VR-related health issues (e.g., nausea). Sagnier et al. (<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 2020" title="Sagnier, C., Loup-Escande, E., Lourdeaux, D., Thouvenin, I., &amp; VallÃ©ry, G. (2020). User acceptance of virtual reality: An extended technology acceptance model. International Journal of Humanâ€“Computer Interaction, 36(11), 993â€“1007. 
                  https://doi.org/10.1080/10447318.2019.1708612
                  
                " href="https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s10639-024-12602-5#ref-CR148" id="ref-link-section-d81076584e2364">2020</a>) have reported that the intention to utilize VR in learning is negatively affected by cybersickness. Consequently, selecting an appropriate resource will encourage learners to benefit better from the VR content, allowing educators to increase their dependency on technology as an assistance tool. Also, suppose a learner is a huge gamer. In that case, the educator could utilize VR or AR learning games to ignite the learnerâ€™s excitement. Accordingly, a positive correlation will be established between employing VR and AR resources and the dependency on technologies as teaching assistance tools. For further clarity, this means that a better selection of VR and AR resources that serve learner needs will increase the dependency level on the technology from the educatorâ€™s side.</p><p>This study also discovered that VR and AR apps will not only reduce educatorsâ€™ efforts but also compensate for the lack of conventional teaching techniques (Li et al., <a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 2021" title="Li, Y., Ye, H., Ye, F., Liu, Y., Lv, L., Zhang, P., &amp; Zhou, Y. (2021). The current situation and future prospects of simulators in dental education. Journal of Medical Internet Research, 23(4), e23635. 
                  https://doi.org/10.2196/23635
                  
                " href="https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s10639-024-12602-5#ref-CR111" id="ref-link-section-d81076584e2370">2021</a>). VR and AR technologies have also increased collaboration between educators and learners (Bistaman et al., <a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 2018" title="Bistaman, I. N. M., Idrus, S. Z. S., &amp; Abd Rashid, S. (2018). The use of augmented reality technology for primary school education in Perlis, Malaysia. In Journal of Physics: Conference Series (Vol. 1019, No. 1, pp. 012064). IOP Publishing.  
                  https://doi.org/10.1088/1742-6596/1019/1/012064
                  
                " href="https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s10639-024-12602-5#ref-CR30" id="ref-link-section-d81076584e2373">2018</a>; Bourguet &amp;  Romero-Gonzalez, <a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 2021" title="Bourguet, M. L., &amp; Romero-Gonzalez, M. (2021). Work-in-progress-teaching invisible phenomena and virtual experiments: immersion or augmentation? In 2021 7th International Conference of the Immersive Learning Research Network (iLRN) (pp. 1â€“3). IEEE.  
                  https://doi.org/10.23919/iLRN52045.2021.9459308
                  
                " href="https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s10639-024-12602-5#ref-CR34" id="ref-link-section-d81076584e2376">2021</a>; Lee, <a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 2020" title="Lee, I. J. (2020). Applying virtual reality for learning woodworking in the vocational training of batch wood furniture production. Interactive Learning Environments, 1â€“19. 
                  https://doi.org/10.1080/10494820.2020.1841799
                  
                " href="https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s10639-024-12602-5#ref-CR109" id="ref-link-section-d81076584e2379">2020</a>). It enables educators to assign responsibilities to learners, allowing them to make their own decisions. Then, discuss the decision with the educator and receive feedback. Consequently, collaboration increases. Furthermore, it helps educators evaluate and observe learnersâ€™ interactions with learning content (Yue et al., <a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 2021" title="Yue, W., Chen, C., Kun, S., &amp; Jinxiu, W. (2021). Research on the application of medical teaching based on XR and VR. In 2021 2nd International Conference on Big Data and Informatization Education (ICBDIE) (pp. 688â€“691). IEEE.  
                  https://doi.org/10.1109/ICBDIE52740.2021.00162
                  
                " href="https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s10639-024-12602-5#ref-CR185" id="ref-link-section-d81076584e2382">2021</a>). This study also discovered that some educators prefer to use one of these technologies in their teaching over another, such as AR technology (Alalwan et al., <a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 2020" title="Alalwan, N., Cheng, L., Al-Samarraie, H., Yousef, R., Alzahrani, A. I., &amp; Sarsam, S. M. (2020). Challenges and prospects of virtual reality and augmented reality utilization among primary school teachers: A developing country perspective. Studies in Educational Evaluation, 66,. 
                  https://doi.org/10.1016/j.stueduc.2020.100876
                  
                " href="https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s10639-024-12602-5#ref-CR8" id="ref-link-section-d81076584e2386">2020</a>). The reasons lie in the ease of observing learnersâ€™ interactions and providing guidance and advice to their learners when using handheld AR compared to VR. In addition, those educators believe that VR educational apps are extremely broad and cannot help monitor their learnersâ€™ interactions. Nevertheless, in this study, the authors found that educators affirmed that VR and AR educational-based apps have great potential to change learning and teaching positively.</p><h3 class="c-article__sub-heading" id="Sec18"><span class="c-article-section__title-number">4.5 </span>RQ5: How do VR and AR educational applications relate to Learning Analytics?</h3><p>Immersive experience has the potential to accumulate a vast amount of data that can be analyzed through Learning Analytics. This analysis can reveal valuable insights into learner interaction, engagement, and performance with the learning content. The extracted data can then be utilized to improve educational experiences by enabling personalized instruction ensuring learning outcomes are attained (Carter &amp; Egliston, <a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 2023" title="Carter, M., &amp; Egliston, B. (2023). What are the risks of virtual reality data? Learning analytics, algorithmic bias and a fantasy of perfect data. New Media &amp; Society, 25(3), 485â€“504. 
                  https://doi.org/10.1177/14614448211012794
                  
                " href="https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s10639-024-12602-5#ref-CR41" id="ref-link-section-d81076584e2398">2023</a>). After an extensive investigation of the relationship between Learning Analytics (LA) and VR and AR technologies, research yielded that few articles (11) had applied the LA technique with VR and AR educational-based applications. This integration resulted in significant advantages. Using LA attempts to comprehend learner interactions and the VR and AR learning environment (Birt et al., <a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 2019" title="Birt, J., Clare, D., &amp; Cowling, M. (2019). Piloting multimodal learning analytics using mobile mixed reality in health education. In 2019 IEEE 7th International Conference on Serious Games and Applications for Health (SeGAH) (pp. 1â€“6). IEEE.  
                  https://doi.org/10.1109/SeGAH.2019.8882435
                  
                " href="https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s10639-024-12602-5#ref-CR29" id="ref-link-section-d81076584e2401">2019</a>).</p><p>This study discovers that assisting in improving teaching and learning (Gorham et al., <a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 2019" title="Gorham, T., Jubaed, S., Sanyal, T., &amp; Starr, E. L. (2019). Assessing the efficacy of VR for foreign language learning using multimodal learning analytics. In C. N. Giannikas, E. K. Constantinou, &amp; S. Papadima-Sophocleous (Eds.). Professional Development in CALL: A Selection of Papers; Research-Publishing. Net: Voillans, France, 101â€“116.  
                  https://doi.org/10.14705/rpnet.2019.28.873
                  
                " href="https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s10639-024-12602-5#ref-CR74" id="ref-link-section-d81076584e2407">2019</a>; Qvist et al., <a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 2015" title="Qvist, P., Kangasniemi, T., PalomÃ¤ki, S., SeppÃ¤nen, J., Joensuu, P., Natri, O., ... &amp; NordstrÃ¶m, K. (2015). Design of virtual learning environments: Learning analytics and identification of affordances and barriers. International Journal of Engineering Pedagogy. 
                  https://doi.org/10.3991/ijep.v5i4.496
                  
                " href="https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s10639-024-12602-5#ref-CR142" id="ref-link-section-d81076584e2410">2015</a>) and helping in recognizing the learnersâ€™ performance (Jiang et al., <a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 2021" title="Jiang, S., Tatar, C., Huang, X., Sung, S. H., &amp; Xie, C. (2021). Augmented reality in science laboratories: Investigating high school studentsâ€™ navigation patterns and their effects on learning performance. Journal of Educational Computing Research, 07356331211038764. 
                  https://doi.org/10.1177/07356331211038764
                  
                " href="https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s10639-024-12602-5#ref-CR93" id="ref-link-section-d81076584e2413">2021</a>; Maiti et al., <a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 2021" title="Maiti, M., Priyaadharshini, M., &amp; Vinayaga Sundaram, B. (2021). Augmented reality in virtual Classroom for Higher Education during COVID-19 pandemic. Intelligent Computing (pp. 399â€“418). Springer.  
                  https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-030-80129-8_29
                  
                " href="https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s10639-024-12602-5#ref-CR118" id="ref-link-section-d81076584e2416">2021</a>; SantamarÃ­a-Bonfil et al., <a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 2020" title="SantamarÃ­a-Bonfil, G., IbÃ¡Ã±ez, M. B., PÃ©rez-RamÃ­rez, M., Arroyo-Figueroa, G., &amp; MartÃ­nez-Ãlvarez, F. (2020). Learning analytics for student modeling in virtual reality training systems: Lineworkers case. Computers &amp; Education, 151, 103871. 
                  https://doi.org/10.1016/j.compedu.2020.103871
                  
                " href="https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s10639-024-12602-5#ref-CR151" id="ref-link-section-d81076584e2419">2020</a>), were the 1st and the 2nd features correlated to applying Learning Analytics with immersive technologies respectively, as shown in Table&nbsp;<a data-track="click" data-track-label="link" data-track-action="table anchor" href="https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s10639-024-12602-5#Tab5">5</a>. While allowing the understanding of learnersâ€™ behavioral characteristics in the learning environment (Jiang et al., <a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 2021" title="Jiang, S., Tatar, C., Huang, X., Sung, S. H., &amp; Xie, C. (2021). Augmented reality in science laboratories: Investigating high school studentsâ€™ navigation patterns and their effects on learning performance. Journal of Educational Computing Research, 07356331211038764. 
